Make DRM an opt-in switch in Settings, off by default.

Aerium does not ship Google's CDM, so it should not tell sites it has one until
somebody asks. This is the arrangement Brave uses, and it is now a switch in
Settings > Privacy and security > Security rather than something decided by
whether a CDM happened to be on disk.

This replaces the chrome://flags entry that was removed a couple of days ago
for not working. That failure is worth recording, because it is what dictates
the shape of this one. On Linux the CDM has to be loaded before the zygote is
sandboxed, so registration runs from ContentMainRunnerImpl's PreSandboxInit and
cdm_registration.cc says as much: "RegisterCdmInfo() will be called by different
processes (the pre-zygote process and the browser process)". chrome://flags
entries become command-line switches much later, in the browser process, so a
SINGLE_VALUE_TYPE flag could never be visible at the moment the decision was
made. Only a real --enable-widevine on the process command line ever worked.

The window that IS still open is the browser process's own registration.
BrowserMainLoop::PreCreateThreads() calls parts_->PreCreateThreads() and then
CdmRegistry::Init(), in that order, and CdmRegistry::Init() is what calls
AddContentDecryptionModules() for the browser. So the pref is read at the end of
ChromeBrowserMainParts::PreCreateThreads(), by which point PreCreateThreadsImpl()
has loaded Local State, and turned into the switch that cdm_registration.cc
reads a moment later.

What that does and does not do, stated plainly rather than implied. On Linux the
zygote has already dlopen'd libwidevinecdm.so before the sandbox closed, and
nothing in the browser process can undo that; the switch does not claim to. What
it controls is whether the browser REGISTERS the CDM, which is what decides
whether any page can request Widevine. Off means no site can use it. On Windows
registration is component-updater driven and happens later, so the same gate
covers loading as well as use.

Local State rather than a profile pref, because PreCreateThreads runs before any
profile exists - that is the whole reason this timing works. settings_private
reaches it anyway: FindServiceForPref() falls through to local_state() for any
pref the profile does not have, so the toggle binds like any other.

Turned into a command-line switch rather than read directly by
cdm_registration.cc, which lives in chrome/common and has no business reaching
into the browser process for a PrefService. It also means every platform reads
exactly one thing - Android sets the same switch from Java before native starts,
where it is early enough to gate registration outright.

The toggle sits on the Security page, next to Manage certificates. That page is
untouched by every other patch in this series, so this hunk sits on upstream
context rather than on context an earlier patch has already rewritten.

